        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Ok thanks 🙏\r\n> \r\n> How can I map the behavior with `mini.visits`?\r\n\r\n@gerazov, I am finally revisiting old-ish issues. Was already implementing the new 'oldfile' behavior in 'mini.bracketed', when it hit me that it *indeed* is possible to achieve jumping through existing buffers based on their recency via 'mini.visits'.\r\n\r\nSlightly modifying [this example from 'mini.visits'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/ecde5ffd9adfd590d5c2d5d35a7f0152def894fc/doc/mini-visits.txt#L304-L317) one can achieve that with something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- NOTE: you may want to use lower/higher `track.delay` to suit your needs\r\nrequire('mini.visits').setup()\r\n\r\n-- Iterate existing buffers for files based on their visit recency\r\nlocal filter_bufexists = function(path_data) return vim.fn.bufexists(path_data.path) == 1 end\r\nlocal sort_latest = MiniVisits.gen_sort.default({ recency_weight = 1 })\r\n\r\nlocal map_iterate_existing = function(lhs, direction, desc)\r\n  local opts = { filter = filter_bufexists, sort = sort_latest, wrap = true }\r\n  local rhs = function() MiniVisits.iterate_paths(direction, vim.fn.getcwd(), opts) end\r\n  vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { desc = desc })\r\nend\r\n\r\nmap_iterate_existing('[{', 'last', 'Existing buffers (earliest)')\r\nmap_iterate_existing('[[', 'forward', 'Existing buffers (earlier)')\r\nmap_iterate_existing(']]', 'backward', 'Existing buffers (later)')\r\nmap_iterate_existing(']}', 'first', 'Existing buffers (latest)')\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo I indeed am going to close this in favor of suggesting to use 'mini.visits' (as it also comes with a few more nice features for this type of use case: persistence across Neovim sessions, customizable delay to register visit, etc.). Sorry for taking so long just to resolve this confusion.",
