        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Going through your code here it seems that you iterate through files in the current directory? This won't work if you have buffers open from different non-nested directories?\n\nIt does not iterate only through files in the current directory but through files *registered* under current directory. 'mini.visits' [uses cwd to register a visit](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/ecde5ffd9adfd590d5c2d5d35a7f0152def894fc/doc/mini-visits.txt#L124-L125) and put it into a human readable index.\n\n> Also, does `mini.visits` jump to the last location in that buffer?\n\nIt shows target buffer in the current window. The last location of that buffer *in current window* is restored, as locations can differ from window to window.\n\n> This could be done with a small mod of your existing function `is_jump_num_from_current_buffer`:\n\nIt would require a bit more than that, I am afraid: an option to configure that behavior and more complicated logic to ensure no infinite recursion. This is not a *huge* deal in isolation, but I don't particularly like that this option significantly changes the behavior of `jump` target which suggests that it should be mapped by default. As there is no good key for that and it would technically be breaking change for existing users, I don't think modifying `jump` target to jump to different buffer is a good idea. Thanks for the PR suggestion, though!",
            "created_at": "2025-01-17T09:19:53Z",
